[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise. They were
merged on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) and tagged by intake
from the reporter's browser. Neither looks at what the report describes, or at the tenant it came
from. Read by tenant and by body, #4471 holds more than one problem, and the queued parser fix
closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket now; the counts are built from the export at
render time.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The problems

### `parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first

Day-first tenants (every tenant on file) get day and month swapped when a date goes through the
shared parser: app, email, and the booking-v2 form. This is the bug #4471 describes, and the one the
queued fix is for.

> "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July." (R-41200, harbour.dental, app)

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first people to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first tenants on booking-v3
(alder.physio, copper.studio, pale.harbour) type what the hint says, and the form reads it the
tenant's way. Every one of these came through the form. The parser fix does not touch them: the
hint, or the form's reading of it, has to change.

> "Customer booked 11 April and got an invite for 4 November." (R-41259, copper.studio, form)

### `one-day`: the date moves by exactly one day

A month/day swap cannot turn 12 May into 11 May. These reports describe the same booking a day
apart, or one day apart for people in different places, and a whole month off by one. That reads
like a time zone or day-boundary bug, not a parser one. The desk noted them (`oneDayMove` in
`desk-notes.json`); the cause table still files them under `parser` by tenant.

> "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting. We held it twice." (R-41344, pale.harbour, email)

> "Every booking in June is one day off. Only June. Why only June." (R-41209, alder.physio, app)

## How this was counted

- One id, one report: the export pages overlap, so repeated ids count once (`uniqueById`).
- Cause by tenant, not by intake tag: locale and booking form from the tenant directory
  (`causeOf`), the way [triage](/kb/triage/dedupe) explains it.
- A report in `oneDayMove` is `one-day`, whatever its tenant.
- The logic is `#lib/kb/split.ts`; the table is `ProblemTable`. When reports keep landing on #4471,
  the counts follow.

## Lessons

- Same subject is not same cause. Count by what the report shows, then merge.
- A footnote under the biggest row is still a problem. Give it a ticket.

